WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.370
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:10.280
If we study the laws of physics and program
them into a computer, we can create a beautiful

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:17.591
simulation that showcases the process of baking,
and if we so desire, when we are done, we

00:00:17.591 --> 00:00:20.360
can even tear a loaf of bread apart.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:27.130
And with this previous method, we can also
smash oreos, candy crabs, pumpkins, and much,

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.160
much more.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.390
This jelly fracture scene is my long-time
favorite.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:38.050
And this new work asks a brazen question only
a proper computer graphics researcher could

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.050
ask - can we write an even more extreme simulation?

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:48.420
I don’t think so, but apparently, this paper
promises a technique that supports more extreme

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:53.230
compression and deformation, and when they
say that, they really mean it.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:58.380
Let’s see what this can do through five
super fun experiments.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.070
Experiment number one.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.070
Squishing.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:07.060
As you see, this paper aligns well with the
favorite pastimes of a computer graphics researcher,

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:12.260
which is, of course, destroying virtual objects
in a spectacular fashion.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:18.540
First, we force these soft elastic virtual
objects through a thin obstacle tube.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.110
Things get quite squishy here…ouch!

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:27.590
And, when they come out on the other side,
their geometries can also separate properly.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:33.230
And watch how beautifully they regain their
original shapes afterwards.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:34.780
Experiment number two.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.140
The tendril test.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:42.500
We grab a squishy ball, and throw it at the
wall, and here comes the cool part, this panel

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.350
was made of glass, so we also get to view
the whole interaction through it, and this

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:50.920
way, we can see all the squishing happening.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:57.810
Look - the tendrils are super detailed and
every single one remains intact and intersection-free

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.930
despite the intense compression.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.620
Outstanding.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.090
Experiment number three.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.220
The twisting test.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:12.080
We take a piece of mat, and keep twisting
and twisting, and… still going.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.390
Note that the algorithm has to compute up
to half a million contact events every time

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:24.480
it advances the time a tiny bit, and still,
no self intersections, no anomalies.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.150
This is crazy.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:31.540
Some of our more seasoned Fellow Scholars
will immediately ask - okay, great, but how

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.510
real is all this?

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:38.210
Is this just good enough to fool the untrained
eye, or does it really simulate what would

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.110
happen in reality?

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:45.410
Well, hold on to your papers, because here
comes my favorite part in these simulation

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:52.010
papers and this is when we let reality be
our judge, and try to reproduce real-world

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:55.280
footage with a simulation.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.099
Experiment number four, the high-speed impact
test.

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:03.720
Here is the real footage of a foam practice
ball fired at a plate.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:09.640
And now, at the point of impact, this part
of the ball has stopped, but the other side

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:13.350
is still flying with a high velocity.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:15.200
So what will be the result?

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.090
A ton of compression.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:23.019
So what does the simulator say about this?

00:03:23.019 --> 00:03:27.150
My goodness.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.060
Just look at that.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.440
This is really accurate.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:31.610
Loving it.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:36.790
This sounds all great, but do we really need
this technique?

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:42.599
The answer shall be given by experiment number
five, ghosts and chains.

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:43.709
What could that mean?

00:03:43.709 --> 00:03:49.989
Here, you see Houdini’s Vellum, the industry
standard simulator for cloth, soft-body and

00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:52.290
a number of other kinds of simulations.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:57.379
It is an absolutely amazing tool, but, wait
a second.

00:03:57.379 --> 00:03:58.489
Look.

00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:05.159
Artificial ghost forces appear, even on a
simple test case with 35 chain links.

00:04:05.159 --> 00:04:10.380
And I wonder if the new method can deal with
these 35 chain links?

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:13.360
The answer is a resounding yes.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.129
No ghost forces.

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:22.639
And not only that, but it can deal with even
longer chains, let’s try a 100 links.

00:04:22.639 --> 00:04:24.759
Oh, yeah!

00:04:24.759 --> 00:04:26.839
Now we’re talking!

00:04:26.839 --> 00:04:29.770
And now, only one question remains.

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:31.480
How much do we have to wait for all this?

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:38.200
All this new technique asks for is a few seconds
per frame for the simpler scenes, and in the

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:42.490
order of minutes per frame for the more crazy
tests out there.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:44.499
Praise the papers!

00:04:44.499 --> 00:04:46.449
That is a fantastic deal.

00:04:46.449 --> 00:04:52.369
And, what is even more fantastic, all this
is performed on your processor, so of course,

00:04:52.369 --> 00:04:57.270
if someone can implement it in a way that
it runs on the graphics card, the next paper

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:04.009
down the line will be much, much faster.

00:05:04.009 --> 00:05:55.189
What a time to be alive!

00:05:55.189 --> 00:06:03.189
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:06:03.189 --> 00:06:04.939
and I'll see you next time!

